[cot:thought] through 2024. Incurred losses will be estimated by multiplying loss ratios against earned premiums, with labels noting approximation where earned data is unavailable.
[cot:thought] Storytelling will
[cot:thought] highlight a hard-market premium surge from 2020 to 2022, followed by a stall and rising loss ratios in 2024.
[cot:thought] Estimated incurred losses are calculated by applying each year???s loss ratio to the corresponding domestic DWP figure.
[cot:thought] Premium growth outpaced
[cot:thought] incurred losses by roughly 157 percent versus 89 percent over the five-year span.
[cot:thought] The 2024 NAIC breakdown shows primary coverage at 65 percent, excess at 31 percent, and endorsement at 4 percent.
[cot:thought] Claim counts reached about 50,000 in 2024, up roughly 40 percent, though closed claims with payment were only 9,941 versus 28,555 without. Ransomware concentration remains unclear because NAIC does not break out insurer losses by that category, and IC3 complaint numbers diverge sharply from actual dollar losses.
[cot:thought] Aon reports that ransomware
[cot:thought] frequency is rising while payouts are declining. The analysis will contrast IC3 threat categories???ransomware, BEC, and personal data breach???to show that BEC dominates external dollar losses while ransomware dominates critical infrastructure complaints. Industry estimates placing ransomware at 50???70 percent of cyber insurance claims will be used with careful labeling.
[cot:thought] Broker reports will
[cot:thought] provide estimated shares for first-party BI and ransomware claims versus third-party and BEC categories.
[cot:thought] NAIC data shows third-party claims rose from 23 percent to 25 percent of reported claims in 2024. Two panels are planned: a dual-axis premium and estimated incurred losses chart, plus a loss ratio time series with soft and hard market bands.
